# Planigons, non-edge-to-edge: how much is the edge-to-edge shelf missing, and can we enumerate it?
# 2026-08-18. AL: "Run tests and explore how planigons tile the plane using non-edge-to-edge
# arrangements."
#
# THE SHIPPED SHELF, for reference: planigon k=1..4 = 18 / 67 / 233 / 749 tilings, all EDGE-TO-EDGE.
#
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# PART 1 — WHICH PLANIGON EDGES CAN BE DIVIDED AT ALL
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# A planigon's edge between the centroids of an n-gon and an n'-gon is the sum of their apothems, so
# the fifteen tiles carry TWELVE lengths, exact in Z[sqrt2, sqrt3] and scaled by 6:
#
#     L12 = 2sqrt3     3.4641      L3  = 6+3sqrt2   10.2426
#     L11 = 3+sqrt3    4.7321      L1  = 6sqrt3     10.3923
#     L9  = 6          6.0000      L8  = 6+4sqrt3   12.9282
#     L10 = 4sqrt3     6.9282      L6  = 9+3sqrt3   14.1962
#     L5  = 3+3sqrt3   8.1962      L2  = 6+6sqrt2   14.4853
#                                  L4  = 6+6sqrt3   16.3923
#                                  L7  = 12+6sqrt3  22.3923
#
# An edge can be met by two neighbours exactly when its length is a SUM of other declared lengths.
# {1, sqrt2, sqrt3} are linearly independent over Q, so this is decidable componentwise and exact.
# SEVEN of the twelve decompose, in 33 ways:
#
#     L1  = L10+L12 = L12+L12+L12
#     L4  = L1+L9 = L5+L5 = L8+L12 = L5+L11+L12 = L9+L10+L12 = L10+L11+L11
#         = L9+L12+L12+L12 = L11+L11+L12+L12                              (10 in all)
#     L5  = L11+L12
#     L6  = L5+L9 = L9+L11+L12 = L11+L11+L11
#     L7  = L4+L9 = L5+L6 = L1+L9+L9 = L5+L5+L9 = ...                     (15 in all)
#     L8  = L5+L11 = L9+L10 = L9+L12+L12 = L11+L11+L12
#     L10 = L12+L12
#
# So the edge-to-edge planigon shelf is a subset, and not a small one: L11 and L12 are the two
# commonest edges on the palette and both appear as parts of a division.
#
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# PART 2 — WHY ONE FLAT CORNER PER DECOMPOSITION IS NOT ENOUGH, AND WHAT IS
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The engine glues half-edge TYPE against half-edge type. Model an L1 edge as [L10|L12] and a
# neighbour presenting a whole L10 edge fits, but a neighbour presenting L12 does not — its edge would
# have to cover half of the L10 segment and there is no such gluing. Model it as [L12|L12|L12] and the
# L12 neighbour fits and the L10 one does not.
#
# The only subdivision under which EVERY arrangement is reachable is the one that cuts every edge of
# every tile into ATOMS — lengths that are not themselves sums — because then every gluing is atom
# against atom and the two sides always line up. The atoms are exactly five:
#
#     L2 = 6+6sqrt2,  L3 = 6+3sqrt2,  L9 = 6,  L11 = 3+sqrt3,  L12 = 2sqrt3
#
# WHAT IT COSTS, and this is the wall. Four lengths have more than one ORDERED atomic composition —
# L8 has 6, L6 has 7, L4 has 10, L7 has 45 — and a tile needs one variant per combination across its
# edges. Full atomisation of all fifteen planigons:
#
#     P6.6.6 1    P8.8.4 1    P12.6.4 140    P12.12.3 1620    P4.4.4.4 1    P6.3.6.3 1
#     P6.4.3.4 4  P6.4.4.3 2  P6.6.3.3 1     P12.3.4.3 36     P12.4.3.3 42
#     P4.3.4.3.3 1   P4.4.3.3.3 1   P6.3.3.3.3 1   P3.3.3.3.3.3 1
#                                                   ------------------------------------------------
#                                                   1,853 tile variants where the palette has 15
#
# All four expensive tiles are the P12.* ones: the dodecagon's apothem is the long one, so L4, L6, L7
# and L8 are the long edges and the long edges are the ones with many ways to be cut.
#
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# PART 3 — THE BOUNDED RUN
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# planigon-split-lite: the ELEVEN planigons whose atomisation needs at most 8 variants, which is every
# planigon that does not touch a dodecagon. 15 tile variants. Generated by
# alphabets/make_planigons_split.py.
#
# DROPPED, and this is a restriction on the answer and not a fact about the plane: P12.6.4 (140),
# P12.12.3 (1620), P12.3.4.3 (36), P12.4.3.3 (42). Tilings using any of those are missing here.
#
# The alphabet, and the first sign of what this costs:
#
#     planigon             18 tiles, 60 classes,  1,104 configs ->  1,821 entries, 0.9 MB tables.bin
#     planigon-split-lite  17 tiles, 85 classes, 84,326 configs -> 86,993 entries,  37.8 MB
#
# A 48x alphabet on 11 of the 15 tiles. For scale, the regular palette reaches k=16 in hours on ~100
# entries, and tri45all-split (717 entries) takes over 20 minutes at k=4.

